Mechanis­m: RSA ciphertext `c` is padded as `0x00 || 0x02 || PS || 0x00 || M`. Bleichenbacher oracle guesses `m = (c * s^e) mod n` and checks padding via timing—OpenSSL 1.1.1o leaks decryption time via `EVP_PKEY_decrypt()`.
Oracle construction:
```c
int oracle(unsigned char *cipher, size_t clen) {
  BIGNUM *c_bn = BN_bin2bn(cipher, clen, NULL);
  int len = EVP_PKEY_decrypt(ctx, plain, &outlen, c_bn, clen);
  BN_free(c_bn); return (len > 0 && plain[0] == 0x02 && plain[1] == 0x??);
}
```
Attack loop: Repeat 2·k times (k = |n| in bits); s values chosen from congruence classes until oracle fires.
Coppersmith recovery: After ~2^20 queries, recovered plaintext is `m = 0x0002||PS||0x00||"secret_key"`; strip PKCS padding to retrieve `secret_key`.
Mitigations: Enforce OAEP padding (`-fips` flag), enable `BN_BLINDING`, and deploy OpenSSL 3.0 with RSA-PSS.
Real-world: ROBOT attack (2017) exploited Bleichenbacher on HTTPS servers; revived in 2022 against RSA-encrypted internal APIs.

